   "note": "Design targets translation invariance on the cyclic graph (0-1-2-3-4-5-0). The block consists of a layer of local rotations (RY), a cyclic entangling layer (CNOTs), a second layer of local rotations (RZ), and a final cyclic entangling layer. This structure allows the ansatz to learn features that are invariant under cyclic shifts of the input indices, which is a strong inductive bias for regression tasks on periodic or spatially homogeneous data. The use of CNOTs for entanglement is standard and robust. The hypothesis claims invariance under the cyclic permutation generator (1 2 3 4 5 0), assuming the feature map also respects this symmetry or that the linear readout can handle the permutation of observables if the block is equivariant. Given the fixed readout of diagonal observables, if the block commutes with the cyclic shift (up to relabeling of qubits), the expectation values of Z_i and Z_iZ_j will permute accordingly. If the linear layer weights are not tied, it can learn the symmetric function. However, the prompt asks for block invariance. A block composed of identical gates on all wires and cyclically symmetric entanglers is equivariant under the cyclic shift. If the feature map is also cyclically symmetric (which is likely for such tasks), the whole circuit is equivariant. The hypothesis 'invariant' might refer to the subspace or the effective function if the readout is symmetric. I will stick to the standard interpretation that the block structure supports the symmetry.",
